WEBVTT

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:48.173
All right. The topic for today is alchemy and active imagination. And I want to start with telling a little bit about Jung because a lot of what Jung has been doing and was interested in was very intertwined with his personal life.

00:00:49.121 --> 00:01:09.107
So Jung had a pretty big secret, a big chunk of his life, which was the secret work on the book, which is now very famous, which is, uh, The Red Book, and he worked from it roughly from age of 38 till 51.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:26.909
And Jung, born in Switzerland in 1875, Swiss psychologist, pretty famous. That's the reason why you're here. Thought it's so strange and so weird to work on this that he basically told no one about it. He went into his fantasies.

00:01:27.503 --> 00:01:49.027
And tried to explore his inner world, and he documented his journey, and he kept journals called the Black Books, which he then brought into a single volume called The Red Book. And The Red Book was really this massive piece that he had hand-drawn and handwritten, and it was bound in red leather. That's why The Red Book.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:02:10.109
And it was basically a family secret. And he showed sometimes some excerpts to his friends and his colleagues and his family, but he did not want anybody to know about it because he found it rather weird. And he worked 13 years on it and almost daily.

00:02:11.425 --> 00:02:34.762
And there, a lot of the ideas what Jung is famous for came from the collective unconscious, the anima, the animus. With shadow, Self, and so on. This whole plethora of ideas came from these investigations into his own psyche and his own fantasies and this documentation. So this was really what filled his nights.

00:02:35.564 --> 00:02:55.489
And during the day, he was a practicing psychotherapist and he also taught and had seminars and so on. So it was a little bit like his second life that he took care of when dinner was done and he would turn to his studies and spend time with himself and his psyche.

00:02:57.144 --> 00:03:19.235
And as I said, this went on for 13 years, so a really long time, until one day he got a letter and a book. And in this package was a translation of an Chinese alchemy text called The Secret of the Golden Flower, and it was translated into German from Chinese.

00:03:20.214 --> 00:03:42.166
And he read it and was completely thrilled by it, and he was asked to write the introduction for it, and he wrote it. And from this point on, his obsession with alchemy started. So he was already in the beginning of 50, and he left the pretty long life till 1961, so roughly 85 years.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:04:03.085
So for the next over 30 years, he invested a lot of time into alchemy and alchemical texts and all the symbols and procedures and so on. So he really became obsessed. And I wrote this also in the description for this event, that this was an obsession of his.

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:26.250
And when you have the Collected Works, There are a lot of alchemical texts, really a lot of alchemical texts and books and everything. And when you have all the stuff by him, it's pretty funny. At a certain point, he just decides to talk about alchemy. He turns into an old man that says, oh, I have some topic I write about. Okay, but now I will talk about alchemy.

00:04:26.315 --> 00:04:48.840
Everything comes back to it in the end. And he has his magnum opus. Which is Mysterium Coniunctionis, which is a very thick book about alchemical texts, and it's about the great unification process. And a part of this very big book, Mysterium Coniunctionis, is Aion.

00:04:49.065 --> 00:05:12.896
Aion was supposed to be like a chapter of it, but it became so big that he had to make it into a separate book. And Aion is pretty famous and hard book to get into, but very, very, very interesting. So tremendous amount of work, a long, long time. He, into his 80s, he wrote all those stuff about alchemy.

00:05:13.492 --> 00:05:37.837
So what did Jung find there? What caught his interest? And what did he think made it so special? This will be what the event will be about. So starting with alchemy, it's incredibly old. It's all, we have some texts from the Greeks who are over 2,000 years old talking about alchemy.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:06:01.279
And the word itself comes from Arabic. And it's a fun thing. You have certain words that start with al-, like alchemy, alcohol, algebra. And al- is in Arabic like the, in Deutsch der Artikel. So it's the kimiya, the koho, the gebra.

00:06:02.848 --> 00:06:20.434
And there already in the name is chemistry, kimiya, in it. And this is really this idea of having different materials and watching how they can transform and change.

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:45.425
And this is a root for chemistry and also a lot of natural sciences. But of course, it's now pretty much disregarded to talk about alchemy as a serious science because this idea of, okay, this alchemist, they tried to turn lead to gold, to become rich. And of course, this does not work.

00:06:45.651 --> 00:06:58.597
Well, you could do it, but it takes an enormous amount of energy to turn lead atoms into gold atoms. So it's like an impossible endeavor.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:15.268
But still, people had a tradition for hundreds, thousands of years where You have these alchemists writing about alchemical stuff and it's very opaque and very hard to understand what they're trying to get.

00:07:16.678 --> 00:07:36.886
And what Jung saw there and was of interest to him was not really this materialistic view to see materials interacting and changing, but rather a psychological view. He was psychologist. He was a psychoanalyst.

00:07:37.689 --> 00:08:00.000
So what he saw there was a unique human product of culture. And Jung was interested in everything. Even when he was very young, he was interested in spirituality, the occult, astrology, history, mythology, fairy tales. He became interested in a lot of fringe topics.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:24.043
And when he got older, he even started to write about UFOs. He took everything that was a part of human experience and human artistic endeavor, cultural endeavor, extremely serious. And he saw always there is something there that draws humans to that, to these topics, to these experiences.

00:08:24.764 --> 00:08:45.657
And he wanted to explore why. So he said, I alchemy, the value in it is not really the science part. This can be disproven pretty easily that you cannot easily turn lead into gold, but rather psychological part. What does it say about the psyche, what they're talking about?

00:08:47.800 --> 00:09:00.717
And alchemy itself, there is no school of alchemy. That he noted, it's not like there were alchemy academies or careers in alchemy.

00:09:01.745 --> 00:09:19.728
It was rather, you had very isolated people, extremely isolated, who started to investigate this material and become incredibly interested in metals and acid and fumes and all those stuff.

00:09:21.158 --> 00:09:38.871
And it's more like a calling, he said, Jung, that people suddenly started to become incredibly interested and pursued this topic. Alchemist always incredibly solitude. And there's one very famous one, and that's Newton.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:53.604
Newton was an alchemist and he was interested in physics also, but like the foundation of Physics, which Newton started, started off as a bet.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:10:18.127
Somebody bet some money that if somebody was able to describe the weird notion that the moons of, I think it was Venus, have, that that person would get money. So Newton worked on it for years and years. Basically to win a bet and start physics.

00:10:18.257 --> 00:10:32.193
So things you do based on a bet. But his main passion was alchemy. So he would put on his little hat and investigate all the stuff that's happening with all the weird materials.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:57.937
And alchemy is based on some premises, which universal to all alchemists, even though that alchemists are not really connected in a direct way. So there's, like I said, there's no schools, there's no career path. There are some very hard to understand texts in very different languages.

00:10:58.515 --> 00:11:21.974
What Jung did his life was to get these texts and translate them from Latin, from Greek, from all different languages. Just to know what they're about. So it's not like there's a unified language. And he noticed certain patterns. Like alchemy, of course, as I mentioned, interest in materials.

00:11:22.071 --> 00:11:41.968
So it's a very outward view. It's a very view into the world, which is already strange in a sense that back then Christianity was rather prevalent. Christianity is very turned inward. Into the salvation of the soul and the spiritual experience of God and so on.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:12:02.715
And Christianity is not so much interested in the outside world, especially Catholicism. We have another tangent I have to go off on. And Catholicism is this idea of the deteriorating world.

