To create a second Adam, which is to some Christian readings of the Bible, also a hermaphrodite until Eve is taken out of his rib and then they are separated. But before, Adam was supposed to be also a hermaphrodite.

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:59.275
So to create something with the lapis, which is perfect, immortal, unchanging, And this is likely where this idea of gold comes from because gold itself is also unchanging. It's like gold does not rust, looks always the same, which is a very unique property of a material because most of things that deteriorate, they rust and so on, but not gold.

00:23:59.852 --> 00:24:22.768
Gold is shiny even when it's thousands of years old. So that makes it valuable because it does not corrupt like a lot of other things. Funny thing, funny note on the side. When you have gold and you have pure gold, it's not shiny. It's rather, uh, dim. Also, you can deform it with your fingers. It's rather soft material.

00:24:22.928 --> 00:24:38.568
So the reason why gold is shiny and strong is because there's a little bit of silver mixed into it. And this is pretty standard. Even if you buy very high quality gold, like gold coins, it's only 99.92% gold. And the rest is silver.

00:24:40.163 --> 00:24:56.950
And to combine all those different things into one thing, a great reunification through the Great Work, through the alchemist and through human beings by taking material things as the highest goal.

00:24:58.611 --> 00:25:20.469
And on the flip side, there's this idea that the lapis can also be found and completely not recognized. So to have something incredibly valuable, which is the goal of everything and the end result of a tremendous work, but it is not recognized.

