Then they talked about the peacock's tail. And if you picture it, how a peacock looks like and the tail with all its feathers and all the colors. This often was what they meant when they saw the different flames, when they just set fire to different things and see it burn differently. Because you have now the color, like in a peacock, in a peacock's tail.

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:44.451
So these are the foundations of alchemy, just to get some basic concepts, how they view the world, the premise they're working on. So now, why was Jung so interested in that? Jung saw there a lot of psychological things happening.

00:38:45.838 --> 00:38:59.308
Because of course, when you have an alchemist and he is locked away in some caves and looking at burning metals and some flames, and he said, this is a lion eating the dragon and so on. These are visions. Those are hallucinations.

00:39:01.577 --> 00:39:21.385
And the interesting thing is the alchemist thought this is happening outside in the material world because they had the focus on the material world, but it was their own psyche being projected on it. This means all those symbols and all those procedures that the alchemists were talking about, they unknowingly talked about their own psychology.

00:39:23.497 --> 00:39:42.896
And this is what Jung did very often when he talked to people who had psychosis, schizophrenia, or dreams, fantasies, and so on, to take note of that and find the commonalities, to find out how the psyche is working in general.

00:39:43.702 --> 00:40:02.496
And here is that the great mishappening took place because alchemy is the start of chemistry. As soon as chemistry got far enough to get results, with all those alchemical nonsense, which could easily be disproven. It was put on the wayside and locked away.

00:40:02.561 --> 00:40:18.433
He said, the chemists are the wrong people for the alchemical texts because it's not about material science. It's about psychology. And the value in alchemy lies in investigating psychology.

00:40:20.503 --> 00:40:41.518
And the great advantage is that the alchemists themselves did not thought about psychology because there was not a concept of psychology and the psyche. It did not exist yet. So they were very naive, just writing everything down, what they experienced to get a very unfiltered view how the psyche is working. And this is something that Jung really, really, really loved.

00:40:44.667 --> 00:41:21.388
He then described that this opus that they did of generating something from the chaos, the ouroboros, to gold, something valuable and incorruptible, as the part of the psyche that creates culture, to have unconscious potential of things that are in the unconscious, which are weird and intermingled and not clearly distinguished, to bring it into the light and transform it into something useful by integrating it into consciousness.

00:41:23.259 --> 00:41:43.862
And these symbols that they're using, they're coming from the collective parts of the psyche. So Jung saw this really as you have your consciousness, this is you, what you know, what you experience, what you can do, and so on. Then you have your personal unconscious, which is all the stuff about you, you don't want to think about, do something about, and so on.

00:41:44.151 --> 00:41:57.345
By a certain point, you hit human nature, and that's the collective unconscious. And this is the layer in the psyche where like all the artistic endeavors come from. Culture comes from stories, mythology, folklore, everything.

00:41:58.728 --> 00:42:11.409
And alchemy being an additional pathway to look into what's happening in the collective unconscious and how the relationships are there.

00:42:13.556 --> 00:42:27.719
And this unification of different contradictory things, he saw really as this basic function of the psyche, of unification.

00:42:29.488 --> 00:42:53.236
This idea of you have your consciousness, which is how you are now, but how you are now is not everything you could be, but you're operating in a stable structure, but the structure is not enough because the world is endless and you are limited. So what is happening through one's lifetime is this constant death and rebirth.

00:42:54.457 --> 00:42:59.476
Of the ego and consciousness to create something new that becomes better and better.

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:23.820
And this is this individuation path that Carl Gustav Jung talked about, of integrating more of the unconscious contents out of the dark part of the psyche, out of the fangs of the dragon, to unify it in the bright shining consciousness, which is very often associated with light. And the sun.

00:43:26.087 --> 00:43:49.458
And one step I missed talking about was when the alchemist worked on their magnum opus to create the lapis, they would encounter figures. And there was one figure which was very often encountered, and that's Mercurius, or Hermes, based on whether Greek or Roman.

00:43:52.170 --> 00:44:13.341
And he's a messenger of the gods and he's a trickster and he's smart and he's like the saint of tradesmen, but also of thieves and so on. A little bit of a shady character. And the alchemists, they would encounter him. And he was like a friend.

00:44:13.438 --> 00:44:32.791
He's a messenger from the beyond, helping him, helping the alchemist. And Jung saw this as a personification of the unconscious, something he noted very often, that the unconscious can take certain personified forms like the shadow, the anima, or the animus, or even the Self.

00:44:34.322 --> 00:44:58.006
That makes it possible for the conscious to interact with the person with this personification and in that way with this part of the psyche, like the anima is the way to the collective psyche, the shadow is the access to the personal unconscious. And this idea of you have Mercurius, where the word curious is coming from, is amazing.

00:44:58.521 --> 00:45:10.556
So the interest is piqued by a messenger that's helping you. And you really have to look out in cities, especially older cities, how often there are statues of Mercurius.

00:45:11.326 --> 00:45:29.388
For a certain time when I was reading these books, I was in the Netherlands a lot, and there are so many buildings where you have this, this head of Mercurius and the wings and the staff, which is also the staff that you have at doctors' offices and so on. It's really tremendous.

00:45:29.741 --> 00:45:51.043
You will find the most insignia of a Greek god from Hermes around, and this idea of curiosity and the messenger of beyond and bringing of wisdom and helping and so on, which is really interesting. So Jung saw that really as a personification of the unconscious.

00:45:52.535 --> 00:46:08.316
So Jung saw that the alchemists were engaging in a psychological endeavor without knowing it. And the technique he described them engaging in is active imagination, which was a technique he came up with.

00:46:09.218 --> 00:46:25.899
So, well, he discovered, rediscovered it basically, but active imagination, which is a second part of this market topic of this evening, is something very very special to Jung.

00:46:28.271 --> 00:46:41.903
It's this, again, this idea you have consciousness where you have light and you have the unconscious where you have basically everything potential coming from and how things change from one area into the next.

00:46:42.998 --> 00:46:58.905
And very often all these things come involuntary, like when you have fantasy, When you have dreams, when you have a hallucination, it's the unconscious pressing itself against consciousness. So it's a rather passive experience.

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:18.577
But what Carl Gustav Jung noted was you can also actively engage with these fantasies that are coming up to basically confront them in, on your own terms, in an active way.

00:47:19.780 --> 00:47:40.257
And this is what he has done when he was writing The Red Book, to have his fantasies and bringing them into a conscious matter and engage with them consciously. He would also encounter personifications and persons that he could talk to and ask questions and get answers. Where he said, okay, this is interesting.

00:47:40.305 --> 00:47:54.744
These are other parts of the psyche that I don't know about, but they have like messengers and I can talk to them. And he used this very often in therapy with his patients.

00:47:56.560 --> 00:48:16.954
For example, when patients got overwhelmed by unconscious material, which was overwhelming them in a, in a passive way, he tried to teach them how to engage with them in an active way to dim it down. And he noted when people would really actively engage with that, that this flood would subside a little bit.

00:48:17.517 --> 00:48:39.439
Because for him, always, it, when the unconscious is pushing against consciousness, it's trying to say something, it's trying to get the attention and bringing the curiosity of consciousness to investigate. But also the other way around, when people would have no unconscious things coming up. For example, they would have no dreams.

00:48:40.228 --> 00:49:01.165
Then he would try to get this communication running so that consciousness is so removed that it does not recognize anything really happening outside of itself. He used it also very often as a concept of amplification for Dream Analysis. I won't go into that too deeply, but it's really just trying to generate more material to analyze from dreams.

00:49:03.794 --> 00:49:10.669
So for active imagination, to do it, it's a skill to be learned.

00:49:11.368 --> 00:49:29.985
And for those people who have experience with meditation, so sitting down, closing your eyes, trying to concentrate and watching yourself, watching your mind, it's a rather hard skill to learn to just sit still and For example, think of nothing.

00:49:31.447 --> 00:49:51.402
When you do it, it's incredibly hard because after a couple of seconds, there's a thought and then you're gone. To just keep it up for a couple of minutes is very, very skillful. It's like not moving when you lie down and say, I don't want to move. It just takes seconds or minutes until it's unbearable and you have to move.

00:49:52.671 --> 00:50:16.082
It's a little bit like that to keep this focus on the fantasy coming up and really holding it still to let it speak all the things it wants to transport. And yeah, to go into it with this attitude to do it voluntarily and actively and taking it seriously, everything that's happening there.

00:50:18.466 --> 00:50:43.001
And being free of criticism to just accept everything. Like the alchemist did, they just accepted everything and they tried to write it down and try to understand it then later on. But really being in this not critical phase, just experiencing everything that is happening. Doesn't matter what is happening. So this is very shortly just active imagination technique by Jung.

00:50:44.338 --> 00:51:03.477
And as you might have noticed, I tried to point out all those different commonalities between Jung and his work and, and alchemy, which explains, at least in my eyes, what his very big interest in alchemy was, because it was matching perfectly his experience.

00:51:03.573 --> 00:51:16.577
Like, he went into hiding to write on his book and write down his experiences. So he didn't do it with materials, but he did it with his psychology.

00:51:18.369 --> 00:51:37.608
And after working 13 years on it with The Red Book, he encountered suddenly this huge group of people who have done it for centuries and millennia and came to very similar experiences as he had. So it's a little bit like a control group.

00:51:38.257 --> 00:51:57.263
So when you, for example, want to find out if a medicine is working, right, you take two groups which are randomly selected and you give it to one group to find, and the other ones you give placebos, and then you try to see from the differences what is working and what is not working.

00:51:58.837 --> 00:52:20.100
And it was another way to get confirmation for all the experiences that he made. And this is where I really think He saw himself very strongly aligned with the alchemist and the alchemical experience, and he saw it later then everywhere.

00:52:20.229 --> 00:52:40.787
He talked about how Faust from Goethe, Part 1 and Part 2, are also basically alchemical texts, especially the second part, which is very hard to understand, as I've heard. The first part is already difficult to understand, but the second part seems to be impossible unless you try to read it as an alchemical treatise.

00:52:43.304 --> 00:53:00.485
So now that I talked so much about alchemy and about Jung, and you're saying, oh, that sounds great, this sounds amazing. When you try to get into alchemy through a Jungian way, as I mentioned, there are a lot of books.

00:53:02.156 --> 00:53:19.580
That Jung has written about it and they're freely available and they're very hard to get into because he's talking about this treatise and you don't, he's too much in detail. It's just like a protocol of all the things he found out when you don't know what he's really trying to get at.

00:53:20.286 --> 00:53:29.271
So when you're interested in alchemy, read the last chapter first, because in the last chapter, he will always say, oh, okay, yeah, this is the essence. He connects it to his own theories.

00:53:30.222 --> 00:53:53.674
And this is also what I tried to do here, to just give a conceptual foundation into, uh, his interest in alchemy and why he was interested and where he saw all those connections, which are for him purely psychological with this process of individuation, of change, of transformation in oneself.

00:53:54.157 --> 00:53:59.608
He noted this creation of the perfect being. Within oneself to create something new.

00:54:01.607 --> 00:54:23.133
And this perfect being in his terminology is the Self, which is the combination, or let's say more the sum of all the things that could be possibly combined and the thing that you could potentially be if you are able to integrate everything which is not integrated. And this you reach through individuation.

00:54:23.542 --> 00:54:45.773
And by bringing unification between conscious and unconscious, which he has his very big opus, his magnum opus, about Mysterium Coniunctionis, which is the mystery of the unification. So incredibly dry, extremely high-level Jung.

00:54:46.144 --> 00:54:58.889
If you want to get into it, have a very strong Jungian foundation, just to not fall asleep. There are a lot of interesting pictures in there. Uh, so they are pretty to look at.

00:55:00.270 --> 00:55:15.389
And yeah, so this is basically everything I could dig up in context of alchemy, Jung's view on it, and my interpretation why I think Jung was so extremely interested in it.

00:55:16.977 --> 00:55:36.418
And I hope you had fun and you learned something, and I'm very thankful and I'm amazed that so many people came for such a fringe topic, which is alchemy. So yeah, I want to say also thanks to you, and this would end the presentation part.

